\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


A positive rational number $q$ is \emph{congruent} if there are positive rational numbers $a,b,c$ with $a^2+b^2=c^2$ and $ab/2=q$; thus $q$ is the area of a right triangle with rational side lengths.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Is every positive integer $n$ with $n\equiv5,6,$ or $7\pmod8$ congruent? \href{https://www.maths.nottingham.ac.uk/plp/pmzcw/download/open.pdf}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does every positive integer in residue class five, six or seven modulo eight occur as the area of a rational right triangle?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 8900009 (AMR-088-0009). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Christian Wuthrich, \emph{Open problems in number theory}, lecture slides (December 2011) slides headed Congruent number problem and Conjecture (following the triangle examples). \url{https://www.maths.nottingham.ac.uk/plp/pmzcw/download/open.pdf}.
\end{description}
